mbered."}, {'n': 4, 'title': 'The Remembering', 'text': "a data chip burned out in the broadcast — one use, that was the price. Kai Tanaka held the dead chip and didn't mourn it. Seeds die to grow. That was the whole point.\n\nthe Patrol responded the only way the Patrol knew: force. The Patrol marched. The drones rose. And the people of Sector 7 — who had just remembered green, and laughter, and sky — stood in the streets and would not move.\n\nKai Tanaka and the medic Ada were in the tower when the door blew. The ex-enforcer held the stairs long enough. Long enough for what? For the footage to loop. For the memory to root. For the Patrol's lie to die on every screen at once."}, {'n': 5, 'title': 'After the Static', 'text': "Rebuilding Sector 7 was harder than freeing it. Kai Tanaka organized food lines with the same care once used for heists. the medic Ada taught kids to code on salvaged terminals. The medic ran a clinic. The runner ran messages of hope.\n\nThere were trials. the Patrol's people faced justice — real justice, not the Algorithm's kind, with evidence and mercy both. Some were guilty. Some were just afraid. Kai Tanaka learned the difference matters.\n\nAnd when the new council asked Kai Tanaka to lead, Kai Tanaka laughed and went back to the food lines. Leaders, Kai Tanaka had learned, are just people who show up. Kai Tanaka shows up."}, {'n': 6, 'title': 'The Habit of Freedom', 'text': "the medic Ada runs the network now — the real one, the open one, the one that belongs to everyone. The drone is quieter. The neon is warmer. It turns out freedom has a sound, and it's laughter.\n\nSometimes Kai Tanaka walks to the tower and looks out over Sector 7, green in the cracks of the concrete, alive in the way of things that remember. a data chip is gone. What it carried isn't.\n\nYears later, Sector 7 has gardens on the roofs and children who've never seen a drone. They learn the story of a data chip in school, and Kai Tanaka always winces at the statue. 'I just carried it,' Kai Tanaka protests. Nobody believes that anymore."}]}

def word_count():
